Who is the most important person in the kingdom?
At that time, Jesus' disciples came to him and asked him, "Who is the most important person in the Kingdom of Heaven?"
Jesus called a little child to him. He placed the child among them.
Jesus said, "What I tell you is true. You must change and become like little children. If you don't, you will never enter the kingdom of heaven."
Everyone who becomes free from arrogance like this child is the greatest in the kingdom of heaven.
Whoever welcomes a little child like this for my sake, welcomes me.
But what if someone leads one of these little ones who believe in me to sin? If he does, it would be better for him to tie a large millstone around his neck and be thrown into the depths of the sea.
How dreadful it is for the world because of the things that lead people into sin! Things like this must come. But how dreadful it is for the one who causes them!
If your hand or foot causes you to sin, cut it off and throw it away. It is better for you to enter the kingdom of heaven with one hand or one foot than to enter hell with two hands and two feet. In hell, the fire burns forever.
If your eye causes you to sin, tear it out and throw it away. It is better for you to enter the kingdom of heaven with one eye than to have two eyes and be thrown into hellfire. The story of the lost sheep.
Make sure you don't underestimate any of these little ones. Here's what I'm telling you. Their angels in heaven can go at any time to visit their Father who is in heaven.
No
What do you think? Imagine a man who owns 100 sheep and one of them goes missing. Doesn't he leave the 99 sheep in the mountains? Doesn't he go out to look for the one that was lost?
What I am going to tell you is the truth. If that sheep is found, it will be happier than the ninety-nine that were not lost.
This is how it is with your father in heaven. He does not want any of these little ones to be lost. When one of them wrongs you
If your brother wrongs you, go to him. Tell him what he did wrong. Keep it between yourselves. If he listens to you, you have won his heart.
But what if he will not listen to you? Then take one or two others along with you. The scripture states: "At the mouth of two or three witnesses every matter shall be established." (Deuteronomy 19:15).
But what if he also refuses to listen to witnesses? Then say it to the church. And what if he refuses to listen even to the church? Do not treat him as your brother. Treat him as you would treat an unbeliever or a tax collector.
What I am going to tell you is the truth. What you shut up on earth will be shut up in heaven. What you open on earth will be opened in heaven.
Again, here is what I say. Imagine that two of you on Earth agree on anything you ask for. My Father in heaven will do it for you.
"Where two or three are gathered in my name, I am there with them." The servant who had no mercy.
Peter came to Jesus and said, "Lord, how many times should I forgive my brother when he sins against me? Is seven times enough?"
Jesus replied, "I tell you, not seven times, but seventy-seven times.
Kingdom of Heaven as a king who wanted to collect all the money that his slaves owed him.
When the king started doing that, a man with millions of dollars in debt was brought to him.
The man was unable to pay. His master issued an order. The man, his wife, children, and all his possessions were to be sold to pay his debt.
The server fell to his knees before him. "Give me time," he pleaded. "I will return everything."
The lady felt sorry for him. She forgave him for what he owed and let him go.
But then that waiter came out and found one of the other waiters who had some dollars on him. He grabbed him and started choking him. "Pay me what you owe me!" he said.
The other slave fell to his knees. "Give me time," he pleaded. "I will pay you back."
But the first servant refused. Instead, he went and had the man thrown into prison. The man would be held there until he could repay his debt.
The other servants saw what happened. This disturbed them greatly. They went and told their master everything that happened.
Then he summoned the master and the first slave, saying, "O wicked slave, I forgave you all that you owed me because you entreated me.
Why did you not show mercy to the other slave as you showed mercy to me?
In his anger, his master handed him over to the guards. He will be punished until he pays all that he owes.
Thus will the heavenly Father deal with you all, if you do not forgive your brother from your hearts.
Jesus teaches about divorce.
When Jesus had finished saying these things, he left Galilee and went into the region of Judea beyond the Jordan River.
Large crowds followed him. He healed them there.
Some Pharisees came to test him. They asked, "Is it lawful for a man to divorce his wife for just any reason?"
Jesus replied, "Haven't you read that at the beginning the Creator 'made them male and female'? (Genesis 1:27)"
The man said, "Therefore a man shall leave his father and his mother, and shall cleave to his wife; and they shall become one flesh." (Genesis 2:24)
They are no longer two, but one. For man shall not separate what God has joined together.
They asked, "Then why did Moses command that a man should give his wife a certificate of divorce and send her away?"
Jesus replied, saying, "Moses allowed you to divorce your wives because of your hardness of heart, but it was not like that from the beginning."
Here is what I say: Whoever divorces his wife and marries another woman commits adultery. A man does not have the right to divorce his wife unless she is not faithful to him.
The disciples said to him, "If this is the case between a husband and wife, it is better not to marry."
Jesus replied, saying: "Not everyone can accept the idea of remaining single. Only those whom fate has helped to live without marrying can accept that."
Some men cannot have children because they were born that way. And some were made that way by others. And others made themselves that way to serve the Kingdom of Heaven. Whoever can accept living that way must do so." The little children are brought to Jesus.
Some people brought little children to Jesus. They wanted him to place his hands on the children and pray for them. But the disciples told the people to stop.
Jesus said, "Let the little children come to me, and do not hinder them, for the kingdom of heaven belongs to such as these."
Jesus laid his hands on them. Then he left from there. Jesus and the Rich Young Man
A man approached Jesus and asked him, "Teacher, what good works must I do to have eternal life?"
"Why do you ask me about what is good?" Jesus answered. "There is only one good God. If you want to enter the kingdom, keep the commandments."
"What then?" the man asked. Jesus answered, "Do not murder. Do not commit adultery. Do not steal. Do not give false testimony."
Honor your father and your mother. (Exodus 20:12-16; Deuteronomy 5:16-20) And "love your neighbor as yourself." (Leviticus 19:18)
The young man said, "I have obeyed all those commands." "What should I do next?"
Jesus came and said, "If you want to be perfect, go and sell all that you have and give to the poor. You will have treasure in heaven. Then come and follow me."
When the young man heard this, he left, sad. He was very rich.
Then Jesus said to his disciples, "What I tell you is true. It is hard for the rich to enter the kingdom of heaven.
Again I tell you, it is difficult for a rich person to enter the kingdom of heaven. Again I tell you, it is easier for a camel to go through the eye of a needle than for a rich person to enter the kingdom of heaven.
When the disciples heard this, they were truly amazed. And they asked, "Then who can be saved?"
Jesus looked at them and said, "With people, it is impossible, but with God, all things are possible."
Peter replied to him, "We have left everything to follow you! What is the reward that will be given to us?"
"What I am about to tell you is true," Jesus said to them. "When all things are renewed, the Son of Man will sit on his glorious throne. Then, you who have followed me will sit on twelve thrones. You will judge the twelve tribes of Israel."
Everyone who has left their home, their family, or their field because of me will receive back one hundred times more than what they left behind. They will also receive eternal life.
But many of the first will be last, and many of the last will be first.
The story of the workers in the vineyard.
The Kingdom of Heaven is like a man who owned a piece of land. He went out early in the morning to hire people to work in his vineyard.
He agreed to pay them their usual wage for a day's work. Then he sent them into his vineyard.
Around nine o'clock in the morning, he went out again. He saw others standing in the marketplace square doing nothing.
He said to them, "You also go and work in my vineyard. I will give you what is fair."
They went off. "He went out again around noon and at three o'clock and did the same thing.
Around five o'clock he went out and saw others still standing around. He asked them, "Why do you stand here all day long and do nothing?"
"Because no one has hired us," they answered. "He said to them, 'Go also you and work in my vineyard.'"
When evening came, the owner of the vineyard spoke to the one who was in charge of the workers. He said, "Call the workers and give them their pay. Begin with the others who were hired recently. Then go to the first ones."
The workers who had resumed work at around five o'clock received their usual daily wage, each one of them.
When the first of those hired arrived, they expected to receive more. But each of them also received their usual daily wage.
When they received that, they began to complain about the owner.
They said: "These people you hired last time only worked for one hour." "I paid them the same amount I paid us. We did most of the work and were in the scorching sun all day."
The owner replied to one of them saying, "My friend, I am dealing with you fairly. Did you not agree to work for the usual day's wage?"
Take your money and leave. I want to give those I recently hired the same salary I gave you.
Do I not have the right to do whatever I want with my own money? Do you feel deceived because I gave others so generously?
Those who are last now will be first, and those who are first now will be last.' Jesus speaks again about his coming death.
Jesus was going up to Jerusalem. On the way, he took the twelve disciples aside to talk to them.
He said, "We are going up to Jerusalem." "The Son of Man will be delivered over to the chief priests and the scribes, and they will condemn him to death."
Then they will hand him over to people who are not Jews. They will mock him and beat him. They will nail him to a cross. And on the third day, he will rise from the dead!" Or a mother asking Jesus a favor
The mother of the sons of Zebedee came to Jesus with her sons. She knelt down and asked him a favor.
"What do you want?" Jesus asked. "Promise me that one of my two sons can sit at your right in your kingdom," she said. "Promise that the other can sit at your left."
Jesus said to them, "You do not know what you are asking. Can you drink the cup of suffering that I am to drink?" "We can," they answered.
Jesus said to them, "You will certainly drink from my cup. But it is not for me to say who will sit at my right hand or at my left. These places belong to those for whom they were prepared by my Father."
The other ten pupils heard about it and were angry with the two brothers.
Jesus called all of them and said, "You know about the rulers of the Gentiles; for they exercise authority over them, and those who are great exercise authority over them.
Don't be like that. Instead, whoever wants to be great among you must be your servant.
And whoever wants to be first, let him be your servant.
Be like the Son of Man. He did not come to be served, but to serve others. He came to give his life as a ransom for many. (Two blind men received their sight)
Jesus and his disciples were leaving Jericho. A large crowd followed them.
Two blind men sitting by the road heard that Jesus was passing by. They cried out, saying, "Lord, Son of David, have mercy on us!"
The crowd ordered them to stop. They were told to be quiet. But the two men cried out even louder, "Lord! Son of David! Have mercy on us!"
Jesus stopped and called them to him. He asked them, "What do you want me to do for you?"
"Sir," they replied, "we want to be able to see."
Jesus felt deep concern for them. He touched their eyes. Immediately they could see. And they followed him.
Jesus enters Jerusalem.
As they all approached Jerusalem, they came to Bethany, which was on the Mount of Olives. Jesus sent two disciples.
He said to them, "Go to the village that is in front of you. As soon as you arrive, you will find a tied-up camel. It will have its little camel with it. Untie them and bring them to me."
If someone tells you something, say the Lord needs them. The owner will send them immediately.
This happened so that what was spoken through the prophet might be fulfilled. It says,
Say to the daughter of Zion, 'Behold, your king is coming to you; righteous and having salvation is he, humble and riding on a donkey, on a colt, the foal of a donkey.' (Zechariah 9:9)
The disciples went and did what Jesus had commanded them.
Bring the donkey and the day horse. They threw their cloaks on them. Then Jesus sat on the cloaks.
A very large crowd gathered and spread their coats on the road. Others cut branches from trees and spread them on the road.
Some people went ahead of him, and others followed. They all cried out: "Hosanna to the Son of David!" "Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord!" (Psalm 118:26) "Hosanna in the highest!"
When Jesus entered Jerusalem, the entire city was stirred up. The people asked, "Who is this?"
The crowd answered, "This is Jesus. He is the prophet from Nazareth in Galilee." Jesus drove them out of the temple
Jesus entered the temple area. He began to drive out all those who were buying and selling there. He overturned the tables of the money changers. He also turned over the benches of those who were selling doves.
He said to them: "It is written that the Lord said: 'My house shall be called a house of prayer.' (Isaiah 56:7) And you have made it a den of robbers." (Jeremiah 7:11)
The blind and the lame came to Jesus at the temple. There he healed them.
The chief priests and the teachers of the law saw the wonderful things he did. They also saw the children in the temple courts shouting, "Hosanna to the Son of David!" When the chief priests and teachers of the law saw this, they began to be very angry.
"Do you hear what these children are saying?" they asked Him. "Yes," Jesus answered. "Have you never read it in the Scriptures: 'I have made you a little lower than the heavenly beings. I have crowned you with glory and honor. I have given you dominion over all other creatures; over the birds of the air, over the cattle, over all the wild animals of the earth, and over every living thing that moves upon the earth.' (Psalm 8:5-8)"
Then Jesus left the people and went out of the city to Bethany. He spent the night there. The fig tree dried up.
Early in the morning, Jesus was on his way back to Jerusalem. He was hungry.
He saw an olive tree by the side of the road. He approached it and found nothing on it but leaves. He said to it, "Never bear fruit!" Suddenly the tree dried up.
When the disciples saw this, they were amazed. And they asked, "How did the tree wither so quickly?"
Jesus replied, saying, "What I am about to tell you is the truth. You must have faith and not doubt; then you will be able to do what was done to the fig tree. And you can say to this mountain, 'Go and throw yourself into the sea'; and it will be done."
"If you believe, you will receive what you ask for when you pray." "Jesus' authority is being questioned."
Jesus entered the temple court. As he taught there, the chief priests and the elders of the people came to him. And they asked him, "By what authority are you doing these things? And who gave you this authority?"
Jesus replied, "I will also ask you a question. If you answer me, I will tell you about the one who sent me to do these miraculous signs."
From where did the baptism of John come? From heaven, or from men? And they began to reason among themselves, saying, "If we say, 'From heaven,' he will say, 'Why then did you not believe him?'"
But what if we say "of the men"? We are afraid of people. Everyone believes that John was a prophet.
They answered Jesus, saying, "We do not know." Jesus said, "Then I will also not tell you by what authority I do these things. The Parable of the Two Sons
What do you think about this? A man had two sons. He went to the first son and said to him, "My son, go and work today in the vineyard."
"I will not," the son answered. But then he changed his mind and went.
Then the father went to the other son. He said the same thing. The son replied, "I will, sir," but he did not go.
"Son of man, who is he who, doing the will of his father, will be able to tell you who the Son of Man is?" "The first," they answered. Jesus said to them, "The very words I speak to you, that is the truth. Tax collectors and prostitutes will enter the Kingdom of God before you do."
John came to teach you the right way to live. And you didn't believe him. But the tax collectors and prostitutes believed in him. You saw that. But even then you didn't turn away from your sins and believe him. The story of the tenants.
Here's another story. There was a man who owned some land, so he planted vineyards. He put up a wall around them. He dug a well for pressing the grapes inside. He also built a watchtower. He conducted the vineyards to some peasants. Then he left on a journey.
When the harvest time approached, he sent his servant to the tenants. And he commanded the servants to gather his share of the fruits.
But the tenants seized his slaves. They beat one of them. They killed another. They threw stones at the third to kill him.
Then the man sent other servants to the tenants. He sent more than he had sent at first. The tenants treated them in the same way.
In the end, he sent his son to them. He said, "They will deal with my son with respect."
But the tenants saw the son coming. They said to each other, "This is the one who will eventually take possession of all the owner's property. Let's kill him. Then everything will be ours."
So they seized him and cast him outside the vineyard. Then they killed him.
When the owner of the vineyard comes back, what will he do to those tenants?
"The evil people will be destroyed," they answered. "Then the vineyard will be let out to other farmers. They will give him his share of the crop at the time of the harvest."
Jesus said to them, "Have you never read in the Scriptures: 'The stone the builders rejected has become the capstone. This was the Lord's doing, and it is marvelous in our eyes'? (Psalm 118: 22, 23)
Here is what I say to you: The kingdom of God will be taken from you and given to those who will produce its fruit.
Everyone who falls on that stone will be shattered into pieces. But the stone will crush anyone who falls on it.
The chief priests and the Pharisees heard reports about Jesus. They realized he was speaking about them.
So they looked for a way to arrest him. But they were afraid of the crowds. The people believed that Jesus was a prophet.
The Wedding Dinner Story
Jesus told them more stories. He said:
Here is how the Kingdom of Heaven is. A king prepared a wedding banquet for his son.
He sent his servant to the guests invited for dinner. The servants told the guests to come, but they refused.
Then he sent out additional servants. "Tell the invited guests," he said, "that I have prepared a wedding feast. I have killed my fattened oxen and my finest livestock. Everything is ready. Come to the wedding feast."
But people did not pay attention. One of them went to his field. And another went to his work.
The rest seized his slaves. They treated them harshly and then killed them.
The king was furiously angry. He sent his army to destroy them. They killed the murderers and burned their city.
Then the king said to his servants, "The wedding feast is ready. But the guests were not worthy of attendance."
Go to the street corners. Invite to dinner anyone you find.
The servants went out into the streets. They gathered all the people they could find, good and bad. Soon the wedding hall was filled with guests.
The king came to visit the guests. He noticed a man there who was not wearing wedding clothes.
My friend, how did you get in here without a wedding outfit? The man found nothing to say.
Then the king said to his servant, "Tie his hands and feet. Throw him out into the darkness. There the people will groan and grit their teeth."
Many are called, but few are chosen. Is it right to pay taxes to Caesar?
The Pharisees went out and schemed to entangle Jesus in his own words.
They sent their followers to him. And they sent with them some Herodians. They said, "Teacher, we know that you are a good man. You teach the way of God truthfully. You do not let others tell you what to do or say. You do not care how important they are."
So then tell us, what do you think? Is it right that we pay taxes to Caesar or not?
But Jesus knew their evil schemes. He said, "Oh, you pretenders to prophecy! Why are you trying to trap me?"
Show me the coin that people use to pay taxes." "Bring him a silver coin."
He asked them: "Whose picture is this? And whose are the words?"
"Caesar," they said. Then he said to them, "Give to Caesar what is Caesar's. And give to God what is God's."
When they heard this, they were astonished. So they left him and went away. Marriage, when the dead rise.
On the same day, Sadducees came to Jesus with a question. They do not believe that people rise from the dead.
" 'Teacher,' they said, 'this is what Moses told us: If a man dies without having any children, his brother must marry his widow. He must have children who will belong to his brother, so his brother will not die without having any children.'"
There were seven of us brothers. The first one got married and died. Since he didn't have any children, he left his wife for his brother.
The same thing happened to the second and third brothers. This continued until it reached the seventh brother.
Finally, the woman died.
Well, when the dead rise, who will her husband be? She married all seven of them.
Jesus replied, "You are wrong, because you do not know the Scriptures. And you do not know the power of God."
When the dead rise, they will not marry. Nor will their fathers give them in marriage. They will be like the angels in heaven.
What about the raising of the dead? Have you not read what God has told you?
He said, "I am the God of Abraham, the God of Isaac, and the God of Jacob." (Exodus 3:6) He is not the God of the dead, but of the living.
When the crowds heard this, they were astonished at his teaching. The most important commandment
When the Pharisees heard that the Sadducees had not been able to answer Jesus, they assembled.
One of them, who was an expert in the law, tested Jesus with a question.
"My teacher," he asked, "what are the most important commandments in the law?"
Jesus replied, saying, "You shall love the LORD your God with all your heart and with all your soul and with all your mind" (Deuteronomy 6:5).
This is the first and most important thing.
And the second is like it: "Love your neighbor as yourself." (Leviticus 19:18)
"All that has been written in the Law and the Prophets is based on these two commandments." "Who is the son of Christ?"
The Pharisees met. Jesus asked them:
What do you think about the Messiah? Whose son is he? "The son of David," they answered.
He said to them, "Why then does David call him 'Lord'? For the Holy Spirit spoke through David himself. David said:
The Lord said to my Lord, "Sit at my right hand until I make your enemies your footstool." (Psalm 110:1)
If David calls him "sir," then how can he be David's son?
No one could answer him with a single word. And from that day on, no one dared to ask him any question again.
Jesus condemns the Pharisees and legal experts.
Jesus spoke to the crowds and his disciples.
"The scribes and Pharisees sit in the seat of Moses," he said.
So you must obey them. Do everything they tell you. But don't do what they do. They don't practice what they preach.
They bind heavy loads and place them on the shoulders of other people. But they are not willing to lift a single finger to move it.
All they do is done so that others may see it. On their foreheads and arms they wear small boxes containing verses from the Koran. They make the boxes very wide. And they make the ribbons on their coats very long.
They love to sit in the place of honor at banquets. They also love to have the most important seats in temples.
They like to be welcomed into the city's markets. They like to be called "Rabbis."
But do not let him call you "Rabbi". You have only one teacher, and you are all brothers.
Do not call anyone on earth 'father'. You have one Father, and He is in heaven.
No one should call you "Teacher." You have one teacher, the Christ.
The greatest among you shall be your servant.
He who exalts himself will be humbled, and he who humbles himself will be exalted.
How terrifying it will be for you, you teachers of religious law, you Pharisees! It is you who are in conflict! It is you who are shutting the door of the kingdom of heaven in people's faces. You yourselves will not enter, nor will you allow those who are entering to go in.
No
How terrible are the things you do, you scholars of the law and you Pharisees! You impostors! You travel over land and sea to make a single convert, and when you have succeeded, you make them twice as much a child of hell as yourselves.
How can you be blind guides? You say, 'If anyone swears by the temple, it means nothing. But if someone swears by the gold of the temple, that must be kept.'
You are blind and foolish! What is the most important thing? Is it gold? Or is it the temple that makes the gold sacred?
You also say, "If anyone takes an oath by the altar, it is nothing; but if anyone takes an oath by the gift on it, he must be bound by his oath."
O ye blind! What is the more important thing? Is it the gift? Or is it the altar which makes the gift sacred?
For anyone who swears by the altar, swears by it and by everything on it.
And everyone who swears by the name of the temple, swears by the name of the temple and by the one who dwells in it.
And everyone who swears by the name of heaven has sworn by the name of God's throne and by him who sits upon it.
How terrible it is, you hypocritical experts in the law and Pharisees! You impostors! You pay God his tenth share of your mint, dill, and cumin. But you neglect the more important matters of the law: justice, mercy, and faithfulness. You should have practiced the latter without neglecting the former.
O guides of the blind! You remove the smallest insect from your food. But you swallow a whole camel!
Woe to you who teach the law and the Pharisees! You hypocrites! You clean the outside of the cup and the dishes, but inside you are full of greed. You are like tombs that look beautiful
Blind Pharisee! First, clean the inside of the cup and the dish. Then the outside will also be clean.
Oh, what an awful thing for you, you scientists in the law and you Pharisees! You fakers! You are like tombs that are painted white. They look beautiful on the outside. But on the inside they are full of the bones of the dead people. They are also full of other things that are not pure and clean.
This applies to you as well. From the outside, it looks like you are doing the right thing. But on the inside, you are full of what is wrong. You pretend to be what you are not.
Woe to you who teach the law and the Pharisees! You impostors! You build the tombs of the prophets and decorate the graves of the righteous.
And she says, "If we had lived in the days of those who preceded us, we would not have done what they did. We would not have helped in the killing of the prophets."
You testify against yourselves and acknowledge that you are the sons of those who killed the prophets.
End the sins of those who preceded you in age!
O nest of toxic lies! How will you escape the judgment of hell?
I will send you prophets and wise men and teachers. You will kill some of them, crucify some of them, and flog some of them in your synagogues and pursue them from town to town.
You will pay with the blood of all the righteous people whose blood has been shed on earth. I mean, from the blood of the righteous Abel to the blood of Zechariah, the son of Berekiah. Zechariah is the one you killed between the temple and the altar.
What I am about to tell you is real. All of this will happen to those who are alive now.
Jerusalem! Jerusalem! You who kill the prophets and stone those sent to you. How often I wanted to gather your children together as a hen gathers her brood under her wings, but you would not let me!
Look, your house is empty.
I tell you, you will not see me again until you say: "Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord." (Psalm 118:26)
End Times Signs
Jesus left the temple. He was walking away when his disciples approached him. They wanted to draw his attention to the temple buildings.
"Do you see all these things?" Jesus asked. "What I tell you is truth. Not one stone here will be left on another. Every stone will be thrown down."
Jesus was sitting on the Mount of Olives. There, the disciples came to him in a deserted place. They said to him, "Tell us, when will this happen? And what will be the sign of your coming? And what will be the sign of the end of the age?"
Jesus answered, "Beware! Take care that no one deceives you.
Many will come in my name. They will say, "I am the Messiah!" They will deceive many people.
You will hear about wars. You will also hear people talking about future wars. Do not worry. These things must happen. But the end is not yet.
Nation will fight against nation. Kingdom will fight against kingdom. And people will be hungry. And there will be earthquakes in many places.
All of this is the beginning of labor pains.
Then people will hand you over to be punished, treated badly, and killed. All nations will hate you because of me.
In that time, many will depart from their faith. They will exchange hatred. They will betray one another.
Many false prophets will appear. They will mislead many people.
Because evil is increasing, the love of most people will grow cold.
But whoever perseveres until the end will be saved.
This good news about the kingdom will be preached in the whole world. You will be a witness for all nations. Then the end will come.
The prophet Daniel spoke of "the abomination that makes desolate" (Daniel 9:27; 11:31; 12:11). One day you will see him, the one who is destined to destroy the city and the sanctuary. The reader must understand this.
Then those who are in Judea should flee to the mountains.
What happens on the surface doesn't land in its home; it takes anything.
Nobody in the field should have to go back for their coat.
How dreadful it would be in those days for pregnant women! How terrifying it would be for nursing mothers!
They prayed that they would not have to flee in winter or on a Saturday.
There will be terrible suffering in those days. It will be worse than at any time since the beginning of the world until now. And there will never be anything like it again.
If time wasn't shortened, no one would survive. But thanks to the chosen people of God, it will be shortened.
At that time, someone might say to you, "Look! Here is the Messiah!" or "He is over there!" Do not believe it.
False christs and lying priests will appear. They will do great signs and wonders. They will try to deceive God's chosen people if possible.
Look, I told you beforehand.
If someone tells you, "He is deep in the desert," do not go there. Or if someone tells you, "He is deep in the house," do not believe him.
Lightning, seen approaching from the east, will be visible in the west. Likewise, when the Son of Man comes, it will be the same.
Vultures will gather wherever a carcass is found.
"Following the terrible suffering of those days, 'the sun will be darkened, and the moon will not give its light; the stars will fall from the sky, and the heavens will be shaken.''" (Isaiah 13:10; 34:4)
Then, the sign of the Son of Man will appear in heaven, and all the nations of the earth will mourn. They will see the Son of Man coming on the clouds of heaven. He will come with power and great glory.
He will send His angels with a loud trumpet sound. He will gather His chosen people from all four directions. He will bring them from the ends of the heavens to their other ends.
Learn a lesson from the fig tree. Once its branches become soft and its leaves sprout, you know that summer is near.
Similarly, when you see all those events happening, you know the end is near. It's right at the door.
What I am about to tell you is real. The people who were living at that time will certainly not die until all these things happen.
The heaven and the earth shall pass away: but my words shall not pass away. No man knows the day or the hour.
No one knows about that day or hour, not even the angels in heaven. The Son does not know; only the Father knows.
Remember how it was in the days of Noah. It will be the same when the Son of Man comes.
In the days before the flood, people were eating and drinking. They were marrying. They were giving their daughters in marriage. They were doing all those things until the day Noah entered the ark.
They had no idea whatsoever what was going to happen until the flood came and swept them all away. That is how it will be when the Son of Man comes.
Two men will be in the field; one will be taken and the other left.
Two women will be ground using a hand millstone. One will be taken and the other will be left.
Therefore beware, you do not know when your Lord will come.
You must understand something. Imagine if the homeowner knew exactly what time of night the thief would come. He would guard the house. He would not let his home be violated.
So you also must be ready. Just as the Son of Man comes at a time that no one expects.
Imagine a master who appoints one of his servants to be responsible for the other servants in his house. The servant's task is to distribute food to them at the proper time. The master desires an honest and wise servant to perform this task.
It would be useful for a servant if his master found him doing his work upon his return.
What I am about to tell you is real. That master will entrust that slave with the management of all that he possesses.
But what if the slave is evil? What if he says to himself, "My master has been away for a long time"?
There is no doubt that if he starts hitting the other servants. And if he drinks and eats with those who drink a lot.
The master of this slave will return on a day the slave does not expect. He will return in an hour the slave does not know.
Then the master will cut him into pieces. He will send him to the place where the plaintiffs go. There they will express their grief and grind their teeth in anger.
The Story of Ten Dolls
Here is how the Kingdom of Heaven will be at that time. Ten virgins took their lamps and went out to meet the bridegroom.
Five of them were ignorant. Five were wise.
The fools took their lamps, but they didn't take any olive oil with them.
The wise ones took oil in bottles with their lamps.
The groom did not come for a long time. All the bride's friends got tired and fell asleep.
At midnight someone shouted, "Here is the bridegroom! Come out to meet him!"
Then all the bride's friends woke up and prepared their lamps.
The ignorant one said to the wise one, "Give us some of your oil. Our lamps are going out."
"No," they answered. "There may not be enough for all of us. Instead, go to those who are selling the oil. Buy some for yourselves."
So they went to buy the oil. But while they were on their way, the groom arrived. The bride's friends, who were ready for the wedding, entered with the groom. Then the door was closed.
The other bridesmaids were late, too. They cried out, "Sir! Sir! Open the door for us!"
But he answered, "What I am going to tell you now is true. I do not know you."
Therefore, be ready. You do not know the day or the hour when the bridegroom will come. The parable of the three workers.
Again, here is how the Kingdom of Heaven will be. A man traveling sent his servants and entrusted them with his possessions.
He gave one thousand dinars to each person. He gave four thousand dinars to the other person. He gave two thousand dinars to the third person. The man gave each servant the amount of money he knew the servant was capable of managing. Then he set off on his journey.
The server who received 10 thousand dollars immediately went and invested his money, earning an additional 10 thousand dollars.
The one who won 4000 dollars won an additional 4000 dollars.
But the man who received 2000 dollars went and dug a hole in the ground. He hid his master's money there.
After a long time, the master of these servants returned. He wanted to collect all the money that they had earned.
The man who received 10 thousand dollars brought the other 10 thousand. He said: "Sir, you placed your trust in me with 10 thousand dollars. Look, I have earned another 10 thousand dollars."
His master replied, "Well done, good and faithful servant! You have been faithful over a little, I will set you over much. Come and share your master's joy!"
The man who had $4000 also came. He said, "Sir, you have deposited $4000 with me. Look, I have earned another $4000."
His master replied, "Well done, good and faithful servant! You have been faithful over a little, I will set you over much. Come and share your master's joy!"
Then came the man to whom he had paid two thousand dollars. He said, "Sir, I knew you were a tough man. You reap where you did not sow. You gather crops where you did not plant the seeds."
I was afraid. I went out and buried the $2000 in the ground. Look, here is your money.
His master replied, saying, "O enemy of thine ignoble, lazy foe! Knowest thou not that I gather where I have not sown? Knowest thou not that I pluck the fruits of harvests where I have sown no seeds?
Well then, you should have put my money in the bank. When I came back, it would have come back to me with interest.
Then his master ordered the other servants saying: "Take from him who has 2000 dollars. Give it to him who has 20000 dollars."
To those who have, more will be given. They will have more than enough. But those who have nothing? Even what little they have will be taken away from them.
Cast that worthless slave out into the darkness. There, in the darkness, people will wail and gnash their teeth. 'Sheep and Goats'
Then the Son of Man will come in his glory. And with him will come all the angels. Then he will sit on his glorious throne. And the people of all nations will be gathered before him.
All nations will gather before him. He will separate people into two groups. He will be like a shepherd separating sheep from goats.
The sheep will be placed on his right, and the goat on his left.
Then the king will speak to those on his right, saying, "Blessed is my Father. Come and seize what belongs to you. For it is the kingdom prepared for you since the creation of the world."
I was hungry. And you gave me something to eat. I was thirsty. And you gave me something to drink. I was a stranger. And you invited me in.
I needed clothes. And you gave them to me. I was sick. And you took care of me. I was in prison. And you came to visit me.
Then the righteous will answer, saying, 'Lord, when did we see you hungry and feed you? And when did we see you thirsty and give you a drink?'
When did we see you as a stranger and invite you in? When did we see you in need of clothes and give them to you?
When have we ever seen you sick or in prison and we went to visit you?
The king will answer, "What I tell you is true. Whatever you did for one of the least of these brothers of mine, you did for me."
Then he says to those on his left: "Cursed! Get away from me into the fire that burns forever. It has been prepared for Satan and his angels."
I was hungry. But you did not give me anything to eat. I was thirsty. But you did not give me anything to drink.
I was a stranger. But you did not let me in. I needed clothes. But you did not give me any. I was sick and in prison. But you did not care for me.
They will also answer: 'Lord, when did we see you hungry or thirsty and did not help you? And when did we see you as a stranger or needing clothes or sick or in prison and did not help you?'
He will say, "What I am about to tell you is the truth. If you do not do anything to the least of these, you will not do it for me."
Then they go away to receive punishment forever. But whoever does good will receive eternal life.
The plan to kill Jesus.
Jesus finished saying all these things. Then he said to his disciples:
You know, Easter is in two days. The Son of Man will be delivered to be crucified on the cross.
Then the chief priests and the elders of the people assembled. They gathered in the palace of the high priest Caiaphas.
They made plans to arrest Jesus cunningly. They wanted to kill him.
But not during the feast," they said. "Or the people may become stirred up." A woman pours perfume on Jesus.
Jesus was in Bethlehem. He was in the house of a man named Simon, who had a skin disease.
A woman came to Jesus with an alabaster jar of very costly perfume. She broke the alabaster jar and poured the perfume on his head as he sat at the table.
When the students saw that, they became angry. They asked, "Why is there such waste?"
The perfume could be sold for a high price. The money could be given to the poor.
Jesus was aware of this. He said to them, "Why do you trouble this woman? For she has done a beautiful thing to me.
There will always be poor people with you. But you will not always have me.
She poured perfume on my body to prepare me for burial.
What I am going to tell you is the truth. What you have done will be told everywhere where this good news is spread throughout the world. It will be told in commemoration of her."
One of the twelve went to the chief priests. His name was Judas Iscariot.
He asked them, "What will you give me if I hand Jesus over to you?" So they counted out for him thirty pieces of silver.
From that time on, Judas watched for an opportune time to deliver Jesus over to them. The Lord's Supper
This was the first day of the Feast of Unleavened Bread. The disciples came to Jesus. They asked, "Where do you want us to prepare for you to eat the Passover meal?"
He answered and said, "Go into the city to a certain man. Say to him, 'The Teacher says, My time is near. I will keep the Passover at your house with my disciples.'"
For the disciples did what Jesus had instructed them. They had prepared the Passover meal.
When evening came, Jesus was at the table with the twelve.
While they were eating, he said, "What I am about to tell you is true. One of you will betray me to my enemies."
The pupils became very sad. They began to say to him, one after the other, "It wasn't me, sir, was it?"
Jesus answered, "The one who dips his hand in the bowl with me will betray me.
The Son of Man will go just as it is written about him. But woe to the one who betrays the Son of Man! It would be better for him if he had never been born.
Joseph was the one who would betray him. He said, "It is not I, my Lord, is it not so?" Jesus's answer: "Yes, it is you."
While they were eating, Jesus took bread. And when he had given thanks, he broke it and gave it to them. And he said to them, "Take, eat; this is my body."
Then he took the cup. He thanked them and handed it over to them. And he said, "Drink from it all of you."
This is my blood of the new covenant. It is poured out to forgive the sins of many.
Here is what I say to you. From now on, I will never drink wine with you again until the day I drink it with you in my Father's kingdom.
Then they sang a hymn and went out to the Mount of Olives. Jesus says Peter will fail.
Jesus said to them, "In this very night, all of you will abandon me." It is written that the Lord said, "I will strike the shepherd, and the sheep will be scattered." (Zechariah 13:7)
But after I am raised from the dead, I will go ahead of you to Galilee.'
Peter replied, "Everyone may leave you, but I never will."
"What I tell you is true," Jesus answered. "This very night before the rooster crows, you yourself will disown me three times."
But Peter said, "I must die with you. I will never say that I don't know you." All the other disciples said the same thing. Jesus Prays in Gethsemane
Then Jesus went with his disciples to a place called Gethsemani. He said to them, "Sit here while I go over there and pray."
Peter and my son Zebdi went with him. He began to feel sad and worried.
Then he said to them, "My soul is very sorrowful, to the point of death. Stay here and keep watch with me."
He went a little further. Then he fell on his face to the ground. He prayed, saying, "My Father, if it is possible, let this cup pass from me. Yet not what I will, but what you will."
Then he came back to his disciples and found them sleeping. "Couldn't you men keep watch with me for one hour?" Peter asked.
Wait and pray. Then you will not fall into temptation when you are tempted. The spirit is willing, but the flesh is weak.
Jesus went out a second time. He prayed, saying, "My Father, if it is possible, let this cup pass from me; yet not what I will, but what You will."
Then he came back. Once again he found them sleeping. They could not keep their eyes open.
So he left them and walked away once more. For the third time, he prayed the same prayer.
Then he went back to the disciples and said to them, "Are you still sleeping and taking your rest? Look, the hour is near. The Son of Man is about to be handed over to the sinners."
Arise! Let us go! Here is he to whom they delivered me!" Jesus is arrested
While Jesus was still speaking, Judas came. He was one of the twelve. A large crowd was with him, carrying swords and clubs. The chief priests and the elders of the people had sent them.
Yosef, who was going to hand Jesus over, had agreed with them on a sign. He said, "The man whom I will kiss is the one; seize him."
Judas immediately went to Jesus and said, "Greetings, Teacher." And he kissed him.
Jesus replied, "My friend, do as you have come for." Then the men approached. They seized Jesus and arrested him.
At that moment, one of Jesus' disciples reached for his sword. He drew it and struck the high priest's servant. He cut off the servant's ear.
Jesus said to him, "Put your sword back in its place. For all who take the sword will perish by the sword."
Do you think I can't ask my father for help? He will send an army of more than 70,000 angels immediately.
But then how are the sacred texts fulfilled? They say it must happen this way.
At that time, Jesus spoke to the crowds, saying, "Is it not led in the group of the armed against you? Should you seize me with swords and sticks to arrest me? I was meeting every day in the courtyard of the temple to teach, and you did not arrest me."
"But all this took place to fulfill the words of the prophets." All his disciples left him and fled. Jesus is taken to the Sanhedrin.
Those who arrested Jesus brought him to the high priest Caiaphas. He gathered there the scholars of the law and the elders.
It wasn't too far away; Peter followed Jesus. He approached the courtyard of the high priest and entered and sat down with the guards to see what would happen.
The chief priests and the entire Sanhedrin were searching for something to use against Jesus. They wanted to kill him.
But they didn't find any evidence, even though many false witnesses appeared. In the end, two more witnesses appeared.
They said, "This man has been claiming, 'I am able to destroy God's temple. I can rebuild it in three days.'"
Then the high priest rose and asked Jesus, "Are you not going to answer? What are these men accusing you of?"
But Jesus remained silent. Then the high priest said to him, "I adjure you by the living God, tell us if you are the Christ, the Son of God."
Yes. Thus it is as ye say, Jesus answered. "But behold, I say unto you all. In the days to come, ye shall see the Son of Man sitting at the right hand of the mighty. And ye shall see the Son of Man coming upon the clouds of the sky."
Then the High Priest tore his clothes, and said, "He has spoken great blasphemy against God! Why do we need any more witnesses? You have heard what he said in his wicked blasphemy.
"What's wrong with you?" "He must die!" was their answer.
Then they threw it in his face. They hit him with their hands. Others slapped him.
They said, "Tell us, O Christ! Who is it that struck you?" Peter says, "I do not know Jesus."
Peter was sitting in the courtyard. A servant girl came to him. She said to him, "You were also with Jesus the Galilean."
But in front of them all, Peter said he wasn't. "I don't know what you're talking about," he said.
Then he went out to the door of the courtyard. There another woman saw him and said to the people, "This man was with Jesus of Nazareth."
Again he said it wasn't so. With an oath he cried: "I don't know the man!"
After a short time, the people who had been standing there approached Peter. "You must be one of them," they said. "The way you talk gives you away."
Then Peter began to curse himself. "I swear, I don't know this man!" he exclaimed. Immediately, a cock crew.
Then Peter remembered what Jesus had said. "The cock will repeat its crowing," Jesus had told him. "Before he does, you will say three times that you do not know me." Peter went out. He broke down and wept bitterly.
Judah hangs himself.
That was early in the morning. All the chief priests and elders of the people decided to kill Jesus.
They bound him and led him away; then they delivered him to Pilate, who was the governor.
Joseph, whom he handed over, saw that Jesus was condemned to death. And he reckoned on what he had done, with deep shame and sorrow. So he gave back to the chief priests the thirty pieces of silver.
"I have sinned," he said. "I handed over an innocent man." "What's it to us?" they replied. "That's your problem."
Then Judas threw the money into the temple and went out. After this, he departed and hanged himself.
The leading priests took the money. "It is sacrilege," they said, "to put this blood money into the Temple treasury. It is the price of a man's death."
They decided to use the money to buy a cemetery field. People from other countries will be buried there.
This is what has made it known until today as the "Field of Blood."
Then the words of the prophet Jeremiah were fulfilled. For he had said, "They took the thirty pieces of silver, the price of my blood, which the people of Israel had valued."
They used the coins to buy the Potter's Field, just as the Lord had instructed me." (Zechariah 11:12, 13; Jeremiah 19:1-13; 32:6-9) "Jesus is brought before Pilate"
Jesus was standing before the governor. The governor asked him, "Are you the king of the Jews?" Jesus answered, "Yes, it is as you say."
But when the leading priests and elders accused him, he made no answer.
Then Pilate asked, "Don't you hear the charges they are bringing against you?"
But Jesus didn't even answer a single charge. The state governor was truly amazed.
It was the Governor's custom at the Easter picnic to pardon one convict. The people got to choose who they wanted.
At that time, they had a notorious prisoner named Barabbas.
When the crowd had gathered, Pilate addressed them: "Whom do you want me to release for you? Barabbas? Or Jesus who is called the Messiah?"
Pilate knew that the leaders were envious. He knew that this was the reason they had handed Jesus over to him.
While Pilate was sitting on the judgment seat, his wife sent him a message. "Have nothing to do with that man," she said, "for he is innocent. I have suffered greatly over this dream I had about this man."
But the chief priests and the elders persuaded the crowd to ask for Barabbas and to have Jesus executed.
"Which do you want me to release?" asked the governor. "Barabbas," they answered.
Then what shall I do with Jesus called Christ?" Pilate asked. And they all answered, "Crucify him!"
"Why? What evil has he done?" Pilate asked. But they shouted all the louder, "Crucify him!"
Pilate saw that he was not making any progress. Instead, the crowd began to get angry. So he took water and washed his hands in front of them. "I am not guilty of the death of this man," he said. "You are the ones responsible!"
Everyone answered: "We and our children will bear the responsibility for his death!"
Pilate released Barabbas, but authorized the flogging of Jesus. Then he handed him over to be crucified. The soldiers mocked Jesus.
The governor's soldiers took Jesus to the palace, which was known as the Praetorium. The rest of the soldiers gathered around him.
They removed his clothes and put a brown cloak on him.
Then they twisted the thorns together to make a crown. They put it on his head. They put a staff in his right hand. Then they fell down on their knees in front of him and mocked him. "We honor you, King of the Jews!" they said.
They sprayed him. And they hit him on the head with a stick over and over again.
After they mocked him, they took off the robe. They returned his clothes to him. Then they led him away to crucify him. Jesus is crucified on the cross.
On their way out of the city, they met a man from Carthage named Simon. They forced him to carry the cross.
They came to a place called Golgotha. The word Golgotha means the place of the skull.
There they mixed the wine with bitter spices and offered it to Jesus to drink. After tasting it, He refused to drink it.
When they seized him and crucified him, they divided his clothes among themselves by casting lots.
Sit down and watch it there.
They placed over his head the title, which read: "This is Jesus, the King of the Jews."
He was stuck with two robbers. One on his right and the other on his left.
Those who passed by scoffed at Jesus and ridiculed him. They shook their heads.
And he said, "So you are going to destroy the temple and rebuild it in three days? Save yourself then! Get down from the cross if you are the son of God!"
Similarly, the priests, the leading scholars, and the elders mocked him.
They said, "Save others." "But he cannot save himself! He is the king of Israel! Let him come down now from the cross! Then we will believe in him."
He wishes to put his trust in God; let God then deliver him now, if He so wills. It is He who said, "I am the Son of God."
Similarly, the thieves who were crucified with Jesus mocked him. Jesus died.
From noon until three o'clock, darkness covered all the land.
About the third hour, Jesus cried out with a loud voice, saying, "Eli, Eli, lama sabachthani?" (Psalm 22:1)
Some of those standing there heard Jesus cry out and said, "He is calling for Elijah."
Immediately, someone ran and brought a sponge. He filled it with sour wine and put it on a reed. He offered it to Jesus to drink.
The rest said, "Leave him alone. Let us see whether Elijah will come to save him."
After Jesus loudly called out again, he died.
At that moment, the temple curtain was torn from top to bottom. The earth quaked. Rocks were split.
The graves were broken. The bodies of many righteous people who died were raised to life.
They came out of the tombs. After the resurrection of Jesus, they entered the holy city. There they appeared to a large number of people.
The Roman centurion and the guards who were with Jesus saw the earthquake and all that had happened. They were terrified. They exclaimed, "Surely he was the Son of God!"
It was not very far, many women were watching. They had followed Jesus from Galilee to meet his needs.
Mary Magdalene was one of them. There was also Mary, the mother of James and Joseph. The mother of the sons of Zebedee was there also. Jesus was buried.
As evening approached, a wealthy man from the city of Arimathea came. His name was Joseph. He had become a disciple of Jesus.
He went to Pilate and asked for Jesus' body. Pilate ordered it to be given to him.
Joseph took the body and wrapped it in a clean piece of cloth.
He placed it in his new grave, which was hewn in the rock. He pushed a large stone in front of the entrance to the grave. Then he left.
Mary Magdalene and the other Mary were sitting there opposite the tomb. The guards of the tomb
The next day was the day after the preparation. The leading priests and the Pharisees went to Pilate.
"Sir," they said, "we recall something that deceiver said while he was still alive. He claimed, 'After three days I will rise again.'"
So he ordered the tomb to be made secure until the third day. If you do not, his disciples may come and take away the body. Then they will go and tell the people that Jesus has come back to life. This last lie will be even worse than the first.
"Take with you some guards," Pilate answered. "Go. Make the tomb as secure as possible."
So they went and secured the tomb. They placed a seal on the stone and put some guards on watch.
Jesus rises from the dead.
It was now Saturday evening. The dawn had come on the first day of the week. Mary Magdalene and the other Mary went to look at the tomb.
There was a strong earthquake. An angel of the Lord descended from heaven. The angel went to the tomb. He rolled back the stone and sat on it.
His body shone like lightning. His clothes were white as snow.
The guards were so afraid of him that they trembled and became like the dead.
The angel said to the women, "Do not be afraid. I know that you are looking for Jesus, who was crucified."
What is here! He is risen, as he said he would! Come and see the place where he was lying.
Hurry! Tell his disciples, "He has been raised from the dead. He is going ahead of you to Galilee. There you will see him." And now I have told you.
The women ran from the tomb. They were afraid but they were filled with joy. They ran to tell the disciples.
Suddenly Jesus met them. "Peace be upon you," he said. They came near him, and held his feet and worshipped him.
Then Jesus said to them, "Do not be afraid. Go and tell my brothers to go to Galilee. There they will see me." The guards reported this to the leading priests.
While the women were on their way, some of the guards entered the city. They reported to the chief priests everything that had happened.
When the high priests met with the elders, they made a plan. They offered the soldiers a large sum of money.
They said to the soldiers: "We want you to say, 'His disciples came at night and stole his body while we were sleeping.'"
If the Governor hears this report, we'll run it. That'll keep you out of trouble.
So the soldiers took the money and did as they were instructed. This story spread everywhere among the Jews to this day. Jesus's last commands to his disciples.
Then the eleven disciples went to Galilee. They went to the mountain to which Jesus had commanded them to go.
When they saw him, they prostrated to him. But some still had doubts.
Then Jesus came to them and said, "All authority in heaven and on earth has been given to me."
Therefore go and make disciples of all nations. Baptize them in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit.
Teach them to obey all that I have commanded you. And you can be sure that I will be with you to the end."
John the Baptist is preparing the way.
This is the beginning of the gospel about Jesus Christ, the Son of God.
A long time ago, the prophet Isaiah wrote: "I will send my messenger before you, who will prepare your way." (Malachi 3:1)
A messenger is calling in the desert: "Prepare the way for the Lord. Make straight paths for him." (Isaiah 40:3)
And so John came. He baptized people in the desert. He also advised that people be baptized and repent of their sins. Then God would forgive them.
All the people from the region of Judea and all the people of Jerusalem went out to him. When they acknowledged their sins, he baptized them in the Jordan River.
John wore clothes made of camel hair. He had a leather belt around his waist. He ate locusts and wild honey.
Here is what John used to preach: "After me will come one who is more powerful than I am. I am not good enough to stoop down and untie his sandals.
I baptize you with water. But he will baptize you with the Holy Spirit." Jesus is baptized and tempted.
At that time Jesus came from Nazareth in Galilee and was baptized by John in the Jordan River.
Jesus was coming up out of the water. At that moment, he saw the heavens being torn open. He saw the Holy Spirit descending on him like a dove.
She spoke to him, a voice from heaven, saying, "You are my son, and I love you. I am very pleased with you."
Immediately the Spirit sent Jesus out into the wilderness.
He was in the desert for forty days. There the devil assassinated him. Wild animals did not harm Jesus at all. Angels took care of him. Jesus chose the first disciples.
After John was put in prison, Jesus went into Galilee. He preached the good news of God.
"It is time," he said. "The Kingdom of God is near. Turn away from your sins and believe the good news!"
One day, Jesus was walking along the shore of the Sea of Galilee. There he saw Simon and his brother Andrew. They were casting a net into the lake. They were fishermen.
"Come, follow me," Jesus said. "I will make you fishers of men."
Immediately they left their nets and followed him.
Then Jesus walked a little farther. And while he was doing this, he saw James the son of Zebedee and his brother John. They were in a boat preparing their nets.
Immediately he called them. They left their father Zebedee in the boat with the hired men. Then they followed Jesus. Jesus Expels an Unclean Spirit
Jesus and his disciples went to Capernaum. And when the Sabbath came, he entered the synagogue. And there he began to teach.
People were astonished by his teachings. He taught them as one having authority. He did not speak as the teachers of the law.
At that very moment, a man cried out in their temple. He was under the control of an evil spirit. He said:
What do you want from us, Jesus of Nazareth? Did you come to destroy us? I know who you are. You are the holy one of God!
"Silence!" Jesus said firmly. "Come out of him!"
The devils violently raged the man. Then she came out of him with a cry.
Everyone was astonished. And they asked one another, "What is this? These are new rules! And with great authority! He even commands evil spirits, and they obey him."
News of Jesus spread quickly throughout Galilee. Jesus healed many people.
Jesus and those with him left the temple and immediately went, together with James and John, to the house of Simon and Andrew.
Simon's mother-in-law was lying in bed. She was suffering from a fever. They told Jesus about her.
He went to her, took her hand and helped her up. The fever left her body. Then she began to serve them.
That evening, after sunset, people brought to Jesus all the sick and all those who were possessed by demons.
All the people in the city gathered at the gate.
Jesus healed many of them. They suffered from all kinds of diseases. He also drove out many evil spirits. But he did not allow them to speak, because they knew who he was. Jesus prays in a quiet place.
It was early in the morning, and the darkness was still over the earth. Jesus arose and went out of the house. He went to a place where he could be alone. There he prayed.
Simon and his friends went to look for Jesus.
When they found him, they cried out, "Everyone is looking for you!"
Jesus replied, "Let us go to another place. I want to go to the nearby villages. I must preach there also. That is why I have come."
He traveled throughout Galilee. He preached in their churches. He also cast out demons. Jesus heals a man with a skin disease
A man with a skin disease came to Jesus. He knelt down and said to Jesus, "If you are willing to make me clean, it is within your power."
Jesus was filled with deep concern. He reached out and touched the man. "I am willing to do this," he said. "Be 'clean'!"
Immediately, the illness left him. He was cured.
He immediately dispatched his Jesus. And he gave the man a stern warning.
"Do not tell this to anyone," he said. "Go and show yourselves to the priest. Make the sacrifices that Moses commanded. This will be proof to the priest and to the people that you are 'clean'."
But the man went out and began to talk at once. He spread the news to all the people. So Jesus was no longer able to enter a city openly. He stayed outside in deserted places. But people still came to him from everywhere.
Jesus heals a man who cannot walk.
After a few days, Jesus returned to Capernaum. The people heard that he had returned to his own town.
Many people gathered until there was no more room. There wasn't even any room outside the door. And Jesus spread the word among them.
Four of those who came were carrying a man who could not walk.
But they could not get him close to Jesus because of the crowd. So they made an opening in the roof above Jesus. Then they lowered the man on a stretcher through the opening.
Jesus saw their faith, and said to the man, "Son, your sins are forgiven."
There were some law professors sitting there. They were thinking.
Why is this person speaking this way? He is saying something very evil! Only God can forgive sins!
Immediately, Jesus knew what they were thinking. He said to them, "Why are you thinking these things?
Is it easier to say to this man, "Your sins are forgiven," or to say, "Get up, take your mat and walk"?
I want you to know that the Son of Man has authority on earth to repent of sins. Then Jesus spoke to the man who could not walk.
"I tell you," he said, "get up. Take your rug and go home."
The man stood up, took his mat, and walked away while everyone was watching. All the people were astonished. They praised God and said, "We have never seen anything like this before!" Jesus Chooses Levi
Again Jesus went out beside the Sea of Galilee. A great crowd came to him. He began to teach them.
As he was walking, he saw Levi son of Alphaeus. Levi was sitting at the tax collector's booth. Jesus said to him, "Follow me." And Levi got up and followed him.
After that, Jesus sat down to eat at the house of Levi. Many tax collectors and sinners were eating with him and his disciples. They were part of the large crowd that was following Jesus.
There were some law professors who were Pharisees. They saw Jesus eating with "sinners" and tax collectors. So they asked his disciples, "Why does he eat with tax collectors and sinners?"
Jesus heard that, and said to them: "Those who are in good health do not need a doctor, but the sick do. I did not come to call the righteous to repentance, but sinners." Jesus is asked about fasting.
The disciples of John and the Pharisees used to fast without eating. Some people came to Jesus and said to him, "The disciples of John fast, and the disciples of the Pharisees also fast. But your disciples are not like that. Why are they not like that?"
Jesus answered, "How can the guests of the bridegroom leave without tasting what he has to offer? They will not fast as long as the bridegroom is with them.
But the time will come when the bridegroom will be taken from them. On that day they will go away without eating.
People do not sew a new piece of cloth onto an old garment. If they did, the new piece would tear away from the old. This would make the holes even worse.
People do not pour new wine into old wineskins. If they do, the wine will burst the wineskins. Then, the wine and the wineskins will both be ruined. No, everyone pours new wine into new wineskins." Jesus is the Lord of the Sabbath.
On a Saturday, Jesus was walking with his disciples through the grain fields. The disciples began to pull out some of the heads of grain.
The Pharisees said to Jesus, "Look! It is unlawful to do this on the Sabbath. Why are your disciples doing this?"
He answered: "Have you never read what David did? He and his men were hungry; they needed food."
This was when Abiathar was the high priest. David entered the house of God and ate the holy bread. Only the priests were allowed to eat it. David also gave some of it to his men.
Then Jesus said to them: "The Sabbath was made for man, and not man for the Sabbath.
"For the Son of Man is Lord even of the Sabbath day."
Healing on Saturday
At another time, Jesus entered the temple. There was a man with a withered and crooked hand.
Some Pharisees were trying to find fault with Jesus. They watched him closely. They wanted to see if he would heal the man on the Sabbath.
Jesus spoke to the man with the withered and deformed hand. "Rise and stand before everyone," he said.
Then Jesus asked them, "What does the Law say about what to do on the Sabbath? Is it right to do good or to do evil? Is it right to save life or to destroy it?" But no one answered him.
Jesus looked at them angrily. He was very angry because their hearts were hardened. Then He said to the man, "Stretch out your hand." He stretched it out, and his hand was restored as new.
Then the Pharisees went out and began to plot with the Herodians against Jesus, seeking to kill him. The crowds followed Jesus.
Jesus went with his disciples to the Sea of Galilee. A large crowd of Galileans followed them.
People heard about all the things Jesus was doing, and many people came to him. They came from Judea and Jerusalem and Idumea. They came from the territories east of the Jordan River. And they came from the region of Tyre and Sidon.
Because of the crowd, Jesus told his disciples to prepare a small boat for him. This would prevent people from gathering around him.
Jesus healed many people. So, the sick were pushed towards him to touch him.
When the people saw those possessed by evil spirits, they fell to the ground in front of him. The spirits cried out, "You are the Son of God!"
But Jesus commanded them not to tell who he was. Jesus appoints the twelve apostles.
Jesus went up a mountain. He called some people to come to him, and they came.
He appointed twelve of them and named them apostles. And from that time they were with him. He also sent them out to preach.
They will have the authority to cast out demons.
Jesus chose the twelve. Simon was one of them. Jesus gave him the name Peter.
There was Jesus son of Zebedee and his brother John. Jesus gave them the name Boanerges, and the word Boanerges means Sons of Thunder.
And there were also Andrew, Philip, Bartholomew, Matthew, Thomas, and James the son of Alphaeus. And there were also Thaddaeus and Simon the Zealot.
Yusuf al-Saqar was also one of them. He was the one who later delivered Jesus to his enemies. Jesus and Baalzebub
Jesus entered a house. Once again a crowd gathered. It was so large that Jesus and his disciples couldn't even eat.
His family heard about it. So, they went to help him. They said, "He has lost his mind."
There were some law professors. They came from Jerusalem. They said, "He is in the control of Beelzebul! He is casting out demons by the power of the prince of demons."
Jesus called his disciples, sat down, and began to tell them stories. He said, "How can Satan cast out Satan?"
If a kingdom fights itself, it will not endure.
If a family splits, it cannot endure.
